1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,080
Equity means you own shares
now, though the share class

2
00:00:03,080 --> 00:00:04,807
determines your rights.

3
00:00:04,807 --> 00:00:08,389
A convertible note is usually a
debt instrument that may convert

4
00:00:08,389 --> 00:00:09,936
into shares later.

5
00:00:09,936 --> 00:00:13,776
It commonly has a principal
amount, interest rate, maturity

6
00:00:13,776 --> 00:00:18,222
date, and conversion terms, but
the actual document controls.

7
00:00:18,222 --> 00:00:22,075
If a note does not convert, the
company may owe repayment,

8
00:00:22,075 --> 00:00:25,451
yet an early-stage company can
lack the cash to pay.

9
00:00:25,451 --> 00:00:29,716
A SAFE is different: it is generally
not debt and does not typically

10
00:00:29,716 --> 00:00:32,294
accrue interest or mature like a
note.

11
00:00:32,294 --> 00:00:37,822
For a simple illustration, a $1,000
note at 6% simple annual interest

12
00:00:37,822 --> 00:00:41,237
for one year would accrue $60
before any repayment or

13
00:00:41,237 --> 00:00:45,387
conversion, assuming its terms
and timing work that way.

14
00:00:45,387 --> 00:00:48,969
This is not a forecast of what an
investor will receive.

15
00:00:48,969 --> 00:00:52,268
Compare current ownership
rights, repayment priority,

16
00:00:52,268 --> 00:00:56,263
maturity, conversion triggers, and
what happens in a sale or

17
00:00:56,263 --> 00:00:57,371
shutdown.

18
00:00:57,371 --> 00:01:01,200
Never assume all convertible
instruments behave alike.

